/** \addtogroup area_algorithm */
/** \defgroup theory_algorithm Theory of other algorithms
 \ingroup area_algorithm

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/algorithm` collect the tools of the
 analysis of algorithms and a few algorithms that illustrate them: the
 solution of the recurrences of section 2.9 of the book (`MasterTheorem.h`,
 `AkraBazzi.h`), the amortized analysis of section 4.2 (`BinaryCounter.h`),
 the elimination of the recursion of section 4.4 (`Hanoi.h`), the general
 schemes of the techniques of design of chapters 12 to 16 (`Paradigms.h`),
 the problems on sequences solved by a scan or by a sort
 (`MaximumSegments.h`, `Subsequences.h`, `SequenceQueries.h`), and the
 algorithms of Monte Carlo of chapter 17 (`MonteCarlo.h`). This page collects
 what the headers take for granted: the notation of the orders of growth (see
 \ref theory_algorithm_notation), the argument behind each solution of a
 recurrence (see \ref theory_algorithm_rec), the two forms of the amortized
 analysis (see \ref theory_algorithm_amort), and, for each family of
 algorithms, the property on which its correctness rests and the argument
 behind its bound (from \ref theory_algorithm_paradigms to
 \ref theory_algorithm_mc). The pseudocode of each algorithm is instead on the
 page of its header, in the comment of its function, together with the
 section of the book, or the paper, that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_algorithm_notation Definitions and notation

 The cost of an algorithm is a function \f$ T( n ) \f$ of the size \f$ n \f$ of
 its input, and it is compared with others by its order of growth:
 \f$ f( n ) = O( g( n ) ) \f$ if \f$ f( n ) \le c \, g( n ) \f$ for some
 \f$ c > 0 \f$ and every \f$ n \f$ large enough,
 \f$ f( n ) = \Omega( g( n ) ) \f$ if \f$ g( n ) = O( f( n ) ) \f$, and
 \f$ f( n ) = \Theta( g( n ) ) \f$ if both hold (chapter 2 of the book). The
 orders that the recurrences of the headers produce are all of the form
 \f[
   c^n \, n^p \, \log^q n \, ( \log \log n )^r , \tag{1}
 \f]
 which `Growth` of `MasterTheorem.h` stores as its four exponents, with a flag
 for a bound that is only an upper one. Two orders (1) compare as their
 functions do for \f$ n \f$ large, i.e., lexicographically on
 \f$ ( c , p , q , r ) \f$, since each factor dominates any power of the
 factors after it; this gives the hierarchy
 \f$ 1 \prec \log n \prec n \prec n \log n \prec n^2 \prec 2^n \f$, and leaves
 out the orders, such as \f$ n^{\log n} \f$, that lie between two families.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ T( n ) \f$</td>
     <td>the cost of an algorithm on an input of size \f$ n \f$, defined by a
         recurrence</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ a \f$, \f$ b \f$, \f$ \alpha = \log_b a \f$</td>
     <td>the number of the subproblems, the factor by which each one is
         smaller, and the exponent that their number and size give</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \beta \f$, \f$ k \f$</td>
     <td>the exponents of the cost \f$ \Theta( n^\beta \log^k n ) \f$ of the
         division and of the combination</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \Phi \f$</td>
     <td>a potential, a function of the state of a structure with
         \f$ \Phi \ge \Phi_0 \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ D[ 0 .. n - 1 ] \f$, \f$ P[ i ] \f$</td>
     <td>a sequence of numbers and its prefix sums
         \f$ P[ i ] = D[ 0 ] + \cdots + D[ i - 1 ] \f$, \f$ P[ 0 ] = 0 \f$,
         so that the segment \f$ D[ b .. s ] \f$ sums to
         \f$ P[ s + 1 ] - P[ b ] \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ K \f$</td>
     <td>the number of independent runs of an algorithm of Monte
         Carlo</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_algorithm_rec Recurrences

 An algorithm of divide et impera that splits an instance of size \f$ n \f$
 into \f$ a \f$ instances of size \f$ n / b \f$, and spends
 \f$ g( n ) = \Theta( n^\beta \log^k n ) \f$ to divide and to combine, costs
 \f[
   T( n ) = a \, T( n / b ) + g( n ) . \tag{2}
 \f]

 <em>Theorem 1 (the master theorem).</em> With \f$ a \ge 1 \f$, \f$ b > 1 \f$
 and \f$ \alpha = \log_b a \f$, the solution of (2) is
 \f$ \Theta( n^\alpha ) \f$ if \f$ \beta < \alpha \f$,
 \f$ \Theta( n^\beta \log^k n ) \f$ if \f$ \beta > \alpha \f$, and, if
 \f$ \beta = \alpha \f$, \f$ \Theta( n^\alpha \log^{k+1} n ) \f$ for
 \f$ k > -1 \f$, \f$ \Theta( n^\alpha \log \log n ) \f$ for \f$ k = -1 \f$ and
 \f$ \Theta( n^\alpha ) \f$ for \f$ k < -1 \f$ (Theorems 2.2 and 2.4 of the
 book).

 Indeed, the tree of the recursion has \f$ \log_b n \f$ levels, and its level
 \f$ j \f$ has \f$ a^j \f$ nodes of size \f$ n / b^j \f$, which cost in all
 \f[
   a^j \, g( n / b^j ) = \Theta\Bigl( n^\beta \bigl( a / b^\beta \bigr)^j
   \log^k ( n / b^j ) \Bigr) . \tag{3}
 \f]
 If \f$ \beta < \alpha \f$, then \f$ a / b^\beta > 1 \f$ and the terms (3)
 grow geometrically towards the leaves, whose \f$ a^{\log_b n} = n^\alpha \f$
 nodes dominate; if \f$ \beta > \alpha \f$ they decrease geometrically and the
 root dominates. If \f$ \beta = \alpha \f$ every level costs
 \f$ n^\alpha \log^k( n / b^j ) \f$, and the sum over the levels is that of
 \f$ i^k \f$ for \f$ i \f$ from 1 to \f$ \log_b n \f$, i.e.,
 \f$ \Theta( \log^{k+1} n ) \f$ for \f$ k > -1 \f$, the harmonic
 \f$ \Theta( \log \log n ) \f$ for \f$ k = -1 \f$, and a convergent series for
 \f$ k < -1 \f$. The floors and the ceilings of the sizes change no order.
 `master()` decides among the five cases, taking as an integer a value of
 \f$ \log_b a \f$ within \f$ 10^{-12} \f$ of one, so that the rounding of the
 logarithms does not move a recurrence from the third case to another.

 When the parts have different sizes, as in
 \f$ T( n ) = T( n / 5 ) + T( 7 n / 10 ) + n \f$ of the selection in linear
 time, the theorem of Akra and Bazzi replaces \f$ \alpha \f$ by the unique
 real \f$ p \f$ with
 \f[
   \sum_{i=1}^k a_i b_i^p = 1 , \qquad
   T( n ) = \Theta\Bigl( n^p \Bigl( 1 + \int_1^n
   \frac{g( u )}{u^{p+1}} \, du \Bigr) \Bigr) , \tag{4}
 \f]
 for \f$ T( n ) = \sum_i a_i T( b_i n ) + g( n ) \f$, \f$ a_i > 0 \f$,
 \f$ 0 < b_i < 1 \f$. The left side of the equation is strictly decreasing in
 \f$ p \f$, from \f$ + \infty \f$ to 0; hence its root exists and is unique,
 and `akraBazziExponent()` brackets it by doubling and halves the bracket 200
 times. With \f$ g( n ) = \Theta( n^\beta \log^k n ) \f$ the integral of (4)
 gives the five cases of Theorem 1 with \f$ p \f$ for \f$ \alpha \f$
 (`akraBazzi()`); in the example \f$ ( 1 / 5 )^p + ( 7 / 10 )^p = 1 \f$ has
 \f$ p < 1 = \beta \f$, and the selection is linear.

 The recurrences that decrease the size by a constant,
 \f$ T( n ) = \sum_{i=1}^h a_i T( n - i ) + \Theta( n^\beta ) \f$, unfold
 instead into a tree of depth \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$: with
 \f$ a = \sum_i a_i = 1 \f$ it is a path of \f$ n \f$ nodes and the solution
 is \f$ \Theta( n^{\beta + 1} ) \f$, while with \f$ a \ge 2 \f$ the tree has
 at most \f$ a^n \f$ nodes and the solution is \f$ O( a^n n^\beta ) \f$
 (Theorem 2.5, `linear()`). The second bound is an upper one only, since the
 branches of different depths make the tree smaller than a full one: the
 recurrence of Fibonacci, with \f$ a = 2 \f$, is
 \f$ \Theta( \phi^n ) \f$, \f$ \phi = ( 1 + \sqrt 5 ) / 2 \f$.

 \section theory_algorithm_amort Amortized analysis

 The amortized cost of an operation is its share of the total cost of any
 sequence of operations, which may be much less than its cost in the worst
 case. The binary counter of \f$ k \f$ bits (`add()` of `BinaryCounter.h`)
 is the example of section 4.2: an increment clears the ones at the bottom
 and sets the first zero, and it changes \f$ k \f$ bits when the counter is
 full of ones.

 <em>Lemma 2 (the binary counter).</em> From a counter of zeros,
 \f$ n \f$ increments change fewer than \f$ 2 n \f$ bits.

 Indeed, by aggregation, the bit \f$ A[ i ] \f$ changes once every
 \f$ 2^i \f$ increments, i.e., \f$ \lfloor n / 2^i \rfloor \f$ times, and
 \f[
   \sum_{i=0}^{k-1} \Bigl\lfloor \frac{n}{2^i} \Bigr\rfloor
   < n \sum_{i=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{2^i} = 2 n . \tag{5}
 \f]
 The same bound comes from the potential \f$ \Phi \f$, the number of ones of
 the counter, which starts at 0 and is never negative: an increment that
 clears \f$ d \f$ bits and sets one costs \f$ d + 1 \f$ and changes
 \f$ \Phi \f$ by \f$ 1 - d \f$, so that its amortized cost
 \f$ d + 1 + \Delta \Phi \f$ is 2, and the total cost is at most \f$ 2 n \f$
 minus the final potential. The method of the potential is the one that the
 other structures of the library use, e.g., the Fibonacci heap and the splay
 tree, since it charges each operation by the state it leaves rather than by
 a count over the whole sequence.

 \section theory_algorithm_paradigms Recursion and the schemes of design

 A recursive procedure runs on the stack of the calls, and it can be made
 iterative by keeping that stack by hand (section 4.4). The towers of Hanoi
 (`Hanoi.h`) move \f$ n - 1 \f$ disks to the spare peg, the largest one to
 its place and the \f$ n - 1 \f$ disks over it, i.e.,
 \f$ T( n ) = 2 T( n - 1 ) + 1 = 2^n - 1 \f$ moves, which is also a lower
 bound, since the largest disk moves only once the others are all on one
 peg. The first iterative version pushes the parameters of the first call
 and turns the second one, which is the last action of the procedure, into a
 change of the parameters (the elimination of a tail call); the second one
 applies the systematic rules, which save with the parameters the label from
 which the caller resumes, and which apply to any recursion.

 The schemes of `Paradigms.h` write the techniques of chapters 12 to 16 once,
 in terms of the parts that make them concrete. A problem of search, of
 decision or of optimization is solved by the enumerative method when its
 search space \f$ Z \f$, an ordered set of objects of which the admissible ones
 are the solutions, can be listed (`enumerateSearch()`, `enumerateOptimize()`);
 backtracking (`backtrack()`, chapter 16) lists it as the leaves of a tree of
 partial solutions, and the pruning of a node that cannot lead to a solution
 removes its whole subtree, which is what makes it faster than the enumeration.
 A greedy algorithm (`greedy()`, chapter 14) fixes one component at each stage
 and never revises it, and it is correct only for the problems where an
 exchange argument shows that some optimum agrees with each choice (see \ref
 theory_optimization_greedy); divide et impera (`divideEtImpera()`, chapter 12)
 costs a recurrence such as (2). With a measure that is only partially ordered
 the answer is the set of the maximal solutions, found by `maximal()` in
 \f$ O( n^2 ) \f$ comparisons.

 \section theory_algorithm_seq Segments and subsequences

 The segment of largest sum of \f$ D \f$ (chapter 2, A Warm-up, of the Pearls
 of Ferragina, `MaximumSegments.h`) is found by four algorithms that differ in
 what they reuse. All the pairs \f$ ( b , s ) \f$ with the sum recomputed cost
 \f$ \Theta( n^3 ) \f$, and with the sum extended from \f$ s - 1 \f$ to
 \f$ s \f$ cost \f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$. The linear scan rests on the following.

 <em>Lemma 3 (the scan of Kadane).</em> An optimal segment has no prefix of
 negative sum; and if every prefix of \f$ D[ b .. s ] \f$ has a nonnegative
 sum, then for each segment that starts at some \f$ b' \in ( b , s ] \f$ the
 one that starts at \f$ b \f$ and ends at the same place has a sum at least
 as large (two properties proved in that chapter).

 Indeed, removing a prefix of negative sum would increase the sum, and
 extending a segment that starts at \f$ b' \f$ back to \f$ b \f$
 adds the prefix \f$ D[ b .. b' - 1 ] \f$, whose sum is not negative. Hence
 the scan of `maxSumSegment()` keeps the sum of the current segment, restarts
 it after the place where it becomes negative, and compares it with the best
 one at each step: \f$ O( n ) \f$ time, one pass over the array, and
 \f$ \lceil n / B \rceil \f$ transfers in a memory of blocks of \f$ B \f$
 items, whatever \f$ B \f$. Equivalently, the best segment ending in
 \f$ s \f$ is \f$ P[ s + 1 ] - \min_{b \le s} P[ b ] \f$, with the minimum
 carried along the scan (`maxSumSegmentPrefix()`); with the length bounded
 to \f$ [ L , U ] \f$ the minimum is over a window of starts that slides to
 the right, which a deque of starts of increasing \f$ P \f$ gives in
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized, since each start enters and leaves it once
 (`maxSumSegmentLength()`).

 The longest segment of sum at least \f$ t \f$ (`longestSegmentAtLeast()`)
 walks, for each end \f$ i \f$, only the starts that could lengthen the best
 segment so far, of length \f$ \ell \f$: they lie left of \f$ i - \ell \f$, and
 among them only the leftmost minima of \f$ P \f$ count, since a start with a
 larger \f$ P \f$ to the right of a smaller one is worse in both respects. The
 chain of those minima, followed from the right, has increasing prefix sums as
 it moves left; the walk
 stops at the first start that fails, and each step past the first one
 lengthens the best segment by one at least. Hence the steps are at most
 \f$ 2 n \f$ in all. The segment of largest average with a length at least
 \f$ L \f$ is a question of slopes: the average of \f$ D[ x .. i - 1 ] \f$ is
 the slope of the segment from \f$ ( x , P[ x ] ) \f$ to
 \f$ ( i , P[ i ] ) \f$, and the best \f$ x \f$ is the point of tangency from
 \f$ ( i , P[ i ] ) \f$ to the lower convex hull of the admissible starts, kept
 by the monotone chain and searched by bisection (`maxDensitySegment()`,
 \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$).

 The longest increasing subsequence of `Subsequences.h` follows the dynamic
 programming of chapter 13 in \f$ O( n^2 ) \f$, with \f$ L[ i ] \f$ the
 longest one that ends in \f$ A[ i ] \f$, and the bound falls to
 \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$ by the following invariant of `lisLength()`: after
 each item,
 \f[
   \mathit{tails}[ \ell ] = \min\{ \text{the last item of an increasing }
   \text{subsequence of } \ell + 1 \text{ items} \} , \tag{6}
 \f]
 a strictly increasing vector, so that the new item \f$ a \f$ extends the
 longest subsequence whose last item is below \f$ a \f$, found by binary
 search, and replaces the next entry. Finally, the questions of
 `SequenceQueries.h` reduce to a sort: the smaller items on the right of each
 one are counted by mergesort, since when an item of the left half is output
 the items of the right half already output are exactly the smaller ones on
 its right; and two sets of points on a line are matched at least cost in
 sorted order, since for \f$ h_1 \le h_2 \f$ and \f$ l_1 \le l_2 \f$ one has
 \f[
   | h_1 - l_1 | + | h_2 - l_2 | \le | h_1 - l_2 | + | h_2 - l_1 | , \tag{7}
 \f]
 and any crossing of an optimal matching can be undone by an exchange.

 \section theory_algorithm_mc Monte Carlo algorithms

 An algorithm of Monte Carlo always ends within its bound, and it may answer
 wrongly with a probability bounded over its random choices, not over the
 inputs (chapter 17 of the book). The three algorithms of `MonteCarlo.h` have
 a one-sided error or an error that \f$ K \f$ runs reduce exponentially.

 <em>Lemma 4 (Schwartz and Zippel).</em> A nonzero polynomial of total degree
 \f$ d \f$ in \f$ n \f$ variables vanishes at a point drawn uniformly from
 \f$ S^n \f$ with probability at most \f$ d / |S| \f$.

 Indeed, for one variable a nonzero polynomial of degree \f$ d \f$ has at most
 \f$ d \f$ roots, and the general case follows by induction on \f$ n \f$,
 writing the polynomial as one in \f$ x_1 \f$ whose leading coefficient is a
 polynomial of the other variables. Hence `zeroPolynomial()`, with
 \f$ S = \{ 1 , \ldots , 2 d \} \f$, errs with probability at most
 \f$ 1 / 2 \f$ per run, and only when it answers zero; \f$ K \f$ independent
 runs leave at most \f$ 2^{-K} \f$. The algorithm of Freivalds
 (`freivalds()`, exercise 17.4) is the same argument for the matrix
 \f$ M = A B - C \f$: if a row \f$ m \f$ of \f$ M \f$ is not zero, say
 \f$ m_j \ne 0 \f$, then for each choice of the other signs of \f$ x \f$ at
 most one of the two signs of \f$ x_j \f$ gives \f$ m \cdot x = 0 \f$, so that
 \f$ M x = 0 \f$ with probability at most \f$ 1 / 2 \f$, and each run costs
 three products of a matrix and a vector, \f$ O( n^2 ) \f$, against the
 product of two matrices. Last, `halfSatisfiable()` (exercise 17.5) estimates
 the fraction of the satisfying assignments by \f$ K \f$ samples; if it is
 \f$ 1 / 2 + \epsilon \f$, the bound of Hoeffding gives
 \f[
   \Pr\Bigl[ \frac{Y}{K} \le \frac{1}{2} \Bigr] \le e^{-2 K \epsilon^2} ,
   \tag{8}
 \f]
 \f$ Y \f$ the number of the true samples, and symmetrically below; the error
 is two-sided, and \f$ K \f$ must grow as \f$ 1 / \epsilon^2 \f$.

 <table>
 <caption>The algorithms of Monte Carlo and their errors</caption>
 <tr><th>Function</th><th>Error</th><th>Probability of error</th>
     <th>Time</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>zeroPolynomial()</tt></td><td>one-sided, on zero</td>
     <td>\f$ 2^{-K} \f$</td><td>\f$ K \f$ evaluations</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>freivalds()</tt></td><td>one-sided, on equal</td>
     <td>\f$ 2^{-K} \f$</td><td>\f$ O( K n^2 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>halfSatisfiable()</tt></td><td>two-sided</td>
     <td>\f$ e^{-2 K \epsilon^2} \f$</td><td>\f$ K \f$ evaluations</td></tr>
 </table>

